\section{RTOS Types}

\subsection{Soft vs. Hard Real Time}

{\bf Real Time:} A computer system which must meet
deadlines in time.

{\bf Soft Real Time:} If the deadlines are not met,
performance is considered low.

{\bf Hard Real Time:} If the deadlines are not met,
the system has failed.

{\bf Super Hard Real Time:} Task length comparable to
clock tick interrupts.

Time is measured by a real time clock:
<hn>We use the terms

``Time Quanta'' ``Time Slice''  ``Ticks''

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\subsection{RTOS Types}

{\tt
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|}
\hline
Type            & Dur. (ticks)      & Task D.L.     & Scheduling \\ \hline
``Super HRT''   &  $\ll 1$          & 1             & Trivial    \\ \hline
``Hard RT''     &  1-5              & 10-20         & Complex    \\ \hline
``Soft RT''     & 10-20             & $\sim 100$    & Complex    \\ \hline
Non RT          &   X               & X             & Trivial    \\ \hline

\end{tabular}
}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\subsection{Scheduling}


At each opportunity, OS asks:
If $N$ tasks are ready, which one should I run?


Scheduler Types:
<hn>\begin{itemize}

<hn>        \item
Infinite Loop
        \begin{itemize}
                \item Most primitive of all
                \item \begin{verbatim}
while(1)
{
task1_fcn();
task2_fcn();
task3_fcn();
...
}
                \end{verbatim}
                \item Each task {\bf must} voluntarily return to
                scheduler quickly.
                \item \begin{verbatim}
taskN_fcn()
{
compute a little bit;
return();
}
                \end{verbatim}
        \end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<hn>        \item
Synchronized While Loop
        \begin{itemize}
                \item the top of the loop waits on a hardware clock.
                \item \begin{verbatim}
while(1)
{
wait(clock_pulse);
task1_fcn();
task2_fcn();
task3_fcn();
...
}
                \end{verbatim}
                \item Each task {\bf must} voluntarily return to
                scheduler quickly, as above.
        \end{itemize}



<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<hn>        \item
Non-preemptive Round Robin
        \begin{itemize}
                \item like initial EE472 labs
                \item Each task {\bf must} voluntarily return to
                scheduler quickly.
                \item \begin{verbatim}
Task()
{
compute a little bit;
return();
}
                \end{verbatim}
        \end{itemize}

<hn>\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\subsection{Schedulers and I/O}

<*hn>
Sometimes tasks in embedded systems do I/O by directly manipulating
hardware. For example, a task may set some bits on a digital output
port. More sophisticated devices such as  disk drives or network
interfaces require complex driver
software and importantly take significant time to respond to commands.
This type of I/O is typically supported by an embedded operating system
which handles all I/O requests through subroutine calls.  Since tasks
using these devices will have to wait for them to respond, the scheduler
is invoked after I/O requests.  If a task is waiting for I/O, the
scheduler will not try to run it unless the I/O is complete.

<*s>
\begin{itemize}
 \item I/O to devices like disk drives and network interfaces is complex
 \item Requires driver software
 \item Requires time
 \item Operating System handles I/O with subroutine calls to driver SW.
 \item After task ``A'' calls for I/O, scheduler runs another task.
 \item Task ``A'' will not be started by scheduler until I/O is ready.
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


<*>
\subsection*{Exercises}

<*hn>
In this part we will study in detail how tasks are selected to run by
various schedulers.  We will see that the amount and timing of CPU
access depends on both the nature of the tasks, and the scheduling
algorithm.  Our tasks are functions like the task of the lab which are
called by the scheduler.  When these functions {\tt return}, the
scheduler starts again.
<*>
Tasks can do three possible things.
\begin{itemize}
        \item Run  - meaning compute in the CPU for a certain amount of
time.
        \item Ask the system for I/O

        \item Return to scheduler.
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\subsubsection*{Diagramming Scheduler Operation}
<*hn>
To study scheduling, print out a ``Scheduler Diagramming Worksheet" from
the EE472 web site. This chart plots time going down the page and CPU
processes (tasks) accross the page. We can think of the horizontal axis
as the memory space of the computer. We will put a shaded box in a
square (or part of a square) when a certain task is running on the CPU.
For example, when the scheduler is running, we would shade under the
``OS'' column. We might also have three tasks labeled A, B, C. We would
shade their columns when they are running. Rember, {\em only one column
may be shaded in each row}.

Assume that each line represents 333 $\mu$s.   Assume that there is
a system clock which generates OS interrups every 1,000 $\mu$s.
To indicate these, make a mark at the left edge of every third
horizontal line.

<*s>

Use on-line PDF scheduler worksheet.

Each Line = 333 $\mu$sec.

Assume a system interrupt every 1,000 $\mu$sec.

Mark interrupts at left edge of every third line.

Each time scheduler runs it requires 100$\mu$sec.

